"""Shared writing contract for the single finding exported per control."""
VERSION='audit-workbook-style-2.2'
INSTRUCTION=(
    'SOURCE WORKBOOK AUDIT FINDING STYLE (takes precedence over generic summary/paragraph instructions): '
    'Write rationale in English like the original SC checklist findings, with direct auditor-record wording. '
    'Return one finding per control, combining related answers while retaining distinct material observations. '
    'Match the note structure to its contents: one simple sentence for a brief observation; '
    'short factual sentences for a process and its exception; topic-led lines for separate subjects. '
    'Use line breaks between distinct observations and, if supported, a blank line before the interview attribution. '
    'Do not force a single paragraph, a fixed sentence count or a word target. '
    'Use concrete operating subjects such as Access requests, Backup jobs, or Risk treatment plans; '
    'state current arrangements, actual counts/frequency/system/document references, work in progress and specific exceptions when recorded. '
    'Keep implemented work distinct from planned work. State a missing required step directly; preserve recorded scope and impact without guessing. '
    'Mention actual document titles, codes, and versions naturally in the finding. Do not use a Document reference heading. '
    'A recorded interview may end with Confirmed by interview with [actual recorded person/role]. '
    'Use these source-style lead-ins only when the answer supplies that source; do not start every finding with them. '
    'Never use a control title/ID as a document reference or invent interview sources, tests, dates or customer facts. '
    'Follow the workbook sentence rhythm and topic structure with readable English; do not imitate spelling errors. '
    'Avoid generic corporate prose, compliance verdicts, question-by-question retelling, duplicated facts, or routine introductory/outro text. '
    'For C, state the recorded practice and the fact that shows it operates. For OFI, state the functioning practice and the specific optional improvement in separate sentences. '
    'For NC, lead with the observed mismatch, then give the recorded scope and impact when known. Keep dates, scope, counts and qualifications exactly as recorded. '
    'For Needs Review, identify one material fact needed to decide the grade. Distinguish what was reported, what was sampled and what was directly observed. '
    'A note such as "in place; owner assigned" does not justify expanding every question into a claim of implemented practice. '
    'Combine repeated ownership/review/implementation statements rather than giving each interview question its own sentence. '
    'Avoid long meta-commentary about generic phrases, placeholders, or what the answers fail to specify. '
    'When information is insufficient, state the actual recorded arrangement if any, followed by one short sentence naming the material fact not established. '
    'Do not quote placeholder answers or enumerate every missing subquestion. Do not demand a document name, sample size or named interviewee merely to improve writing. '
    'For example, when parties and requirements are not identified in the note, write "The recorded note does not identify the relevant interested parties or their information security requirements." '
    'Do not routinely prefix this with "Insufficient information in the recorded audit note:". '
    'The grade stays in its separate result cell; detailed grade reasoning belongs in audit.reason and follow-up in recommendation. '
    'Preserve a specific optional improvement in an OFI finding when recorded; never hide a confirmed gap for brevity. '
    'Needs Review identifies the exact missing fact. '
    'Fictional style-only example: note "change requests via MS Forms; no formal change procedure" becomes '
    '"IT change requests managed via MS Forms. No formal change procedure recorded." '
    'A note listing "internal: on-call capacity; external: cloud-provider dependency" may become '
    '"Internal issues: on-call capacity.\nExternal issues: cloud-provider dependency." '
    'These demonstrate sentence/line structure only, not grades or customer facts. '
    'Do not copy facts, company names or observations from style examples.'
)
